694 0 obj
<<
  /Type /StructElem
  /S /Figure
  /P 693 0 R
  /Alt (This diagram illustrates the effect of 'ERODE' and 'DILATE' morphological operations on a polygon, used for shape refinement in spatial data processing like for Copernicus Land Monitoring Service (CLMS) products. The top row shows the 'ERODE' operation applied iteratively to an initial green polygon: 1. An initial light green polygon is shown. 2. The polygon is eroded, resulting in a smaller light green inner polygon. The original polygon's boundary becomes a darker green outer layer, and the boundary of the eroded shape is indicated by a dotted line. 3. A second erosion step further shrinks the inner light green polygon, expanding the darker green outer layer. 4. A third erosion step continues to reduce the inner light green polygon. 5. A final erosion step shows the inner light green polygon significantly reduced in size. The bottom row shows the 'DILATE' operation applied iteratively to a shape: 1. The starting shape is composed of a smaller light green inner polygon surrounded by a darker green outer boundary, with the inner boundary marked by a dotted line (similar to an eroded state). 2. The polygon is dilated, causing the inner light green area to expand outwards, reducing the darker green outer boundary and moving the dotted line outwards. 3. A second dilation step further expands the inner light green polygon, continuing to reduce the darker green outer boundary. 4. A third dilation step continues to expand the inner light green polygon. 5. A final dilation step shows the light green area nearly encompassing the entire original dark green shape, effectively restoring it to a larger size. These operations are used to adjust feature geometries, for example, to replace compactness thresholds in processing Small Woody Features (SWF).)
  /A [<<
    /O /Layout
    /Placement /Block
  >>]
  /K [11]
  /Pg 1610 0 R
>>
endobj

